WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:07.270
PepeFi is a testnet prototype for trading the price of crypto, gold and US stocks, without buying them.

00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:09.084
A trader wants all three.

00:00:09.084 --> 00:00:10.080
Can he have them?

00:00:11.080 --> 00:00:12.082
Crypto first.

00:00:12.082 --> 00:00:19.330
Twenty mock dollars down, at two times leverage: his position moves like forty dollars of ETH.

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:22.980
The price comes from a bot the team runs, on a schedule.

00:00:23.980 --> 00:00:26.700
On the real site, the order fills on-chain.

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:32.840
Now gold: a real-world-asset market, behind a velvet rope.

00:00:33.190 --> 00:00:37.130
Entry needs a qualified-investor pass, proof you may trade it.

00:00:37.130 --> 00:00:39.990
Here, the team's own test wallet issues them.

00:00:40.340 --> 00:00:41.460
The trader has none.

00:00:42.460 --> 00:00:43.451
The app says no.

00:00:43.451 --> 00:00:45.310
The order button stays locked.

00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:48.464
So he steps over the rope.

00:00:48.464 --> 00:00:52.050
He skips the app and sends the order straight to the contract.

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:55.090
But the contract has the same guard at its door.

00:00:56.960 --> 00:01:01.562
The explorer keeps the receipt: failed, not KYC-verified.

00:01:01.562 --> 00:01:03.580
Same wallet, same answer.

00:01:04.580 --> 00:01:06.608
Stocks have a rule of their own.

00:01:06.608 --> 00:01:10.220
When the US market is closed, they switch to reduce-only:

00:01:10.570 --> 00:01:12.430
no new positions while it's closed.

00:01:13.430 --> 00:01:16.260
On the site, the US stocks wear the badge.

00:01:17.930 --> 00:01:19.330
Two locks, one rule.

00:01:19.330 --> 00:01:21.430
The app's lock can be skipped.

00:01:21.430 --> 00:01:22.900
The contract's can't.

00:01:26.330 --> 00:01:33.690
A testnet prototype: mock dollars, no real assets, and investor passes the team issues itself.
